  "executive_briefing": "As of mid-2026, global corporate tax regimes and international trade policies are defined by the operationalization of the OECD Pillar Two 15% global minimum tax, heightened trade friction and legal challenges surrounding tariff policies, and accelerated adoption of digital transaction controls. Over 140 Inclusive Framework jurisdictions continue integrating GloBE model rules, backed by updated OECD Corporate Tax Statistics reporting. Simultaneously, unilateral executive tariffs and subsequent court rulings under the International Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA) generate supply chain uncertainty and prompt trilateral trade reviews. Regionally, ASEAN economies maintain statutory rate stability\u2014such as Singapore's 17% baseline coupled with 2026 corporate tax rebates\u2014while establishing firm statutory timelines for Peppol-based InvoiceNow mandates stretching toward 2031.",
